00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:15.769
The Wuhan Coronavirus began, like all new
viruses, with a remarkably ordinary moment.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:22.830
In late-November, 2019, someone—it is not
yet known who—ate a wild animal that, by

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:27.720
chance, carried the novel coronavirus, and
days later, started to feel the symptoms of

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.840
what initially seemed like a bad cold.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.510
From there, more individuals were infected—at
first through the consumption of other wild

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.010
animals, but later through direct human-to-human
transmission.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.100
For the entire month of December, the virus
was there, in Wuhan, spreading, but nobody

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.100
knew it.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.480
The cases, presenting as Pneumonia, were at
first considered to be caused by any one of

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.239
the countless other viruses that can create
such symptoms, but eventually, the escalation

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:59.780
in cases, and the fact that almost all of
the first patients had bought food at one

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.909
particular wild animal market, caught the
Wuhan city government’s attention.

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:09.299
The first official confirmation that something
was up came on New Year’s Eve, December

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:13.510
31st, when the Municipal Health Commission
published a press release acknowledging the

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.869
situation—over a month after the first transmission.

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:23.399
At the time, this was small, local news—only
a few dozen cases of Pneumonia in a city of

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:26.280
millions, in a country of a billion.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.659
But then came the news that this was not one
of the many pre-existing Pneumonia-causing

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:32.200
viruses.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.780
This was something new.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.429
Western media first reported on the novel
Coronavirus around January 8th.

00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:43.979
The New York Times, forebodingly, wrote, “there
is no evidence that the new virus is readily

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.229
spread by humans, and it has not been tied
to any deaths.”

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:49.890
That would soon change.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.490
On January 21st, when the World Health Organization
started tracking the outbreak, there were

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.940
282 cases.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:09.340
The next day: 314; the next: 581; then 846;
then 1,320; then 2,014; and by the start of

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:15.450
the week of January 26th, it was clear that
this would not be a quick and painless virus.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.981
This would be one for the history books, and
it was going to get a lot worse before it

00:02:18.981 --> 00:02:21.260
got any better.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.030
Drastic circumstances called for drastic action.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.819
It was clear, given the trends, that Wuhan’s
hospitals would soon be overwhelmed by patients

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:34.180
and, in the case of highly infectious viruses
like Coronavirus, that makes a bad situation

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.950
worse as infected individuals will try to
recover at home—where the virus can more

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.250
easily spread.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:45.950
Therefore, the decision was made, on January
23rd, to build a brand new facility capable

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.819
of treating 1,000 patients at once—Huoshenshan
hospital.

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:55.959
It was announced that this would be done,
fully constructed, by February 2nd—just

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:58.580
ten days later.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.610
China has experience with such a feat.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:07.299
In 2003, the country suffered an outbreak
of severe acute respiratory syndrome, or SARS,

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:10.150
which is also caused by a form of Coronavirus.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.040
Much like with the novel Coronavirus, hospitals
were overwhelmed and many had to turn away

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.040
patients.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.790
In response, the government decided to build
a new hospital.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:25.180
Over six days, 7,000 construction workers
brought the Xiaotangshan hospital into reality,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.560
and it quickly began accepting patients and
relieving the overstressed medical system.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.799
Once the outbreak settled down, the Xiaotangshan
hospital was hailed as a massive success by

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:36.610
both the country itself and the World Health
Organization.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.150
The statistics speak for themselves.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.830
Overall, SARS had a fatality rate of 9.6%.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:49.370
At the Xiaotangshan hospital, just 1.2% of
patients died and not a single staff member

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.370
was infected.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.050
It’s no wonder why China wanted to replicate
this and therefore Xiaotangshan was the model

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.000
for the new coronavirus hospital, but this
time would be different.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.319
The 2003 hospital began construction over
six months after the beginning of the outbreak.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.319
The 2020 hospital started construction less
than two months after the first case and just

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:12.550
days after the seriousness of the outbreak
became clear.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.019
There was far less time for planning and design.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.020
The orders came, and then it was immediately
time to start.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:23.270
Step one was finding a place to put the hospital.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.400
The location selected was previously just
an open field, about 14 miles or 22 kilometers

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.680
from central Wuhan.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.100
This kept it away from the dense urban core,
where further transmission might be easier,

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:39.130
but the field in question was also right off
a large, preexisting road which made access

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.370
easy.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.730
That road was quickly shut down and turned
into, essentially, a parking lot and staging

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.230
area for the project.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.630
The contract for this project was given to
China Construction Third Engineering Bureau

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:55.370
Company—a subsidiary of the state-owned
China State Construction Engineering Corporation.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.390
By revenue, this is the world’s largest
construction company and it has experience

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:04.170
in enormous projects like Beijing’s new
airport, Egypt’s new capital city, and Tesla’s

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.670
Shanghai gigafactory.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.980
If anyone was going to pull this off, it was
them.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:14.000
To begin construction in earnest, they first
needed to prepare the selected site.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.470
That involved, first, cutting down all the
trees and shrubbery, and then perfectly leveling

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.680
the ground.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:25.360
Out of the 10-day construction timeline, this
took longer than one might think—about 48

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.360
hours.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.920
This was due in part to the fact that the
project was significantly understaffed in

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.250
its first few days as they scrambled to find
workers, especially as its start coincided

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.070
with Chinese New Year’s.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:40.040
Once the land was leveled, though, they moved
on to constructing the building foundations

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.340
by Sunday, January 26th.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.950
For this, they used a layered system.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.630
On the bottom, an 8 inch or 20 centimeter
layer of sand was placed, then what’s referred

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:55.020
to as a geotextile layer,—a form of fabric—then
a layer of waterproof plastic, then another

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.520
geotextile layer, then another layer of sand.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.350
On that, they then poured concrete which dried
over a few days.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:09.050
But then came the truly impressive element—constructing
the buildings themselves in a matter of days.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:13.410
What made this possible was the use of prefabricated
elements.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.310
Modular construction such as this has been
on the rise recently.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:21.990
It’s now a well-established technique for
the construction of buildings large and small.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.860
The idea is, simply, that somewhere offsite
a number of identical building elements are

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:27.860
built.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.550
Then, they’re shipped to the construction
site and put together.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.300
This has plenty of advantages.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:39.000
First and foremost, the use of identical elements,
built in a factory system, to construct a

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.330
larger building lowers cost through economies
of scale.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.800
Then, when you actually get to putting the
building together, on-site construction time

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.050
is significantly less, which once again lowers
cost.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:56.270
In this case, though, cost was not the primary
concern—speed was, and modular construction

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.910
is the fastest way to build.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:03.680
Most the site used a type of prefabricated,
modular construction common with temporary

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:04.680
buildings.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.250
While it’s not clear which company produced
the prefabricated components, it is clear

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.830
that they existed before the project started.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.400
The first building elements started coming
together on Monday, January 27—just four

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.490
days after the project broke ground.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.520
They began by building the skeleton of the
modular units.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.680
Each of these units was about the shape and
size of a 20-foot shipping container, and

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.610
this skeleton form included just the floor,
ceiling, and structural supports at each corner.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.470
Throughout this all, separate teams, out of
the thousands of workers there, started to

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:38.960
hook the site up to utilities like sewage,
water, electricity, and internet.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.210
Much of this just involved bringing in the
state-owned companies responsible for these,

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:48.020
but for internet, and the subsequent building-out
of an IT system, private companies like Huawei

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:49.800
and Lenovo got involved.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:53.940
This was, no doubt, partially because the
construction of this hospital drew considerable

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.810
public attention in China.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.090
It came to represent the physical manifestation
of the country’s response to the crisis

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.900
and also of China’s engineering might.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:06.770
The construction process itself was streamed
by state-owned broadcasting companies and

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.560
up to 18 million concurrent viewers watched
the process.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.920
Nearly every major broadcaster in China reported
from the site, publishing footage like what

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:19.090
you’re seeing now, although very few western
broadcasters were there.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:23.330
This is part of the reason limited detailed
information and footage of the construction

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:28.120
exists outside of China’s state-owned broadcasters—where
the government can manage the story and what

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.120
gets out.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.380
It was, no doubt, viewed as an opportunity
by the Chinese government in a time when it

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:37.529
faced intense criticism for its initial response
to the outbreak.

00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:42.219
But of course, to gain that praise, the project
needed to be successful.

00:08:42.219 --> 00:08:47.080
On Tuesday, January 28th, significant portions
of the first building were completed, while

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.779
the rest of the complex’s foundations were
finishing up.

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:55.189
Over the 29th, and 30th, more of the overall
structure came to be, as the walls were installed

00:08:55.189 --> 00:08:59.810
on and in the skeletons of buildings, and
work then began on turning these empty buildings

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:01.210
into a hospital.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:06.209
Each patient room was outfitted with two beds,
some medical equipment, and a bathroom.

00:09:06.209 --> 00:09:10.579
Considering the hospital is built with quarantine
in mind, there are some unique aspects to

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:12.480
the design of these rooms.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.430
For one, the ventilation system is built to
keep negative pressure in all quarantine areas

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.480
so that air will go out through an exhaust
system, rather than out under the door into

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.970
the rest of the hospital.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:26.860
While, at the time of writing, it’s not
entirely known to what extent this Coronavirus

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.279
can spread through airborne transmission,
a ventilation system like this is standard

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:34.060
practice for any quarantine ward.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.680
In addition to the individual patient rooms,
separate areas were designed for patient processing,

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.350
diagnostic tools like CT scanners were installed,
and other crucial aspects like offices, break

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.889
rooms, and canteens were built in.

00:09:45.889 --> 00:09:50.810
Over the 31st of January and 1st and 2nd of
February, the final areas of the complex were

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:55.600
built up, the interior installation was wrapped
up, and all the systems were tested to assure

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.210
that the hospital was ready to bring in patients.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:02.610
Meanwhile, eight People’s Liberation Army
transport planes landed at Wuhan airport carrying

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.611
the military medical staff who would work
the hospital.

00:10:05.611 --> 00:10:09.590
They moved into the facility throughout the
same day—February 2nd—then throughout

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:13.950
the night, workers put the finishing touches
onto the hundreds of rooms.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:19.850
Then, the next morning, the moment that ten
days prior seemed impossible arrived—the

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:24.810
first patients, transferred from other facilities,
were wheeled into Huoshenshan hospital and

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:26.980
thus marked the beginning of its operations.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:32.100
They started with just a few dozen patients,
but in a matter of days this ballooned to

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.100
hundreds.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:37.700
Huoshenshan Hospital was only the start of
China’s plan to ramp up the number of available

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.499
beds in Wuhan.

00:10:39.499 --> 00:10:44.350
Just three days after Huoshenshan’s opening,
another, even larger hospital called Leishenshan

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:45.630
opened.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.410
Its 1,600 beds and 32 wards were built in
just twelve days.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:54.800
Meanwhile, other hospitals were converted
to isolate and treat Coronavirus patients

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:58.579
and stadiums and exhibition centers were turned
into hospitals as well.

00:10:58.579 --> 00:11:03.759
Still, though, even with this swift, decisive
action, reports indicate that many of those

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:09.189
with milder cases of Coronavirus are getting
turned away from hospitals in Wuhan.

00:11:09.189 --> 00:11:14.649
It unfortunately seems that Coronavirus, with
its tremendous severity, is one of the few

00:11:14.649 --> 00:11:18.560
things that can outpace China.

